From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Omer Dangoor <omerdn1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 02:35:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fix(android): let waitToSettleTimeoutMs 0 skip the settle
 after a tap too

0007 made waitToSettleTimeoutMs 0 skip the window settle loop that runs
before a tap, where the flow's app id is passed. After the tap,
Maestro.hierarchyBasedTap calls waitForAppToSettle again without an app
id, and AndroidDriver sends that to ScreenshotUtils.waitForAppToSettle,
which reads the view hierarchy twice to compare them. Each read first
waits on the device for the accessibility event stream to go quiet for
500 ms (MaestroDriverService.refreshAccessibilityCache), and right after a
tap that stream is busy, so a tap that asked for no settle wait still
spends most of a second there. With retryTapIfNoChange false, Maestro's
default, there is only one attempt, so the comparison decides nothing.

With waitToSettleTimeoutMs 0, AndroidDriver.waitForAppToSettle now returns
null with or without an app id and asks the device nothing. A positive
timeout, or none, settles as before.

hierarchyBasedTap counts a null settle as a change, so on its own that
would stop a tapOn with retryTapIfNoChange: true from ever tapping a
second time. When another attempt may follow, hierarchyBasedTap now reads
the hierarchy once itself and compares, so the retry still sees an
unchanged screen. The last attempt reads nothing, so a quick tap without
retryTapIfNoChange still reads no hierarchy after the tap.
